# Database dumps. beacon.dump/ holds pg_dump output named by the time the dump
# was TAKEN, not the time it was filed -- a dump's value is entirely in which
# state of the database it captures.
#
# These carry users.password_hash and api_keys.key_hash, and the loose one sat
# untracked AND unignored in the repo root for days, one `git add -A` from
# being in history forever. So the globs matter more than the directory rule,
# which only protects dumps someone remembered to file.
#
# Suffixes, not a directory: nothing in this repo produces dumps (no pg_dump
# target in the Makefile, no caller in the tree), so every one is typed by
# hand and lands wherever the operator is standing. `*.dump` alone missed the
# obvious variants -- a gzipped dump, a second copy, pg_dump's .backup
# convention. Bare `*.sql` is deliberately NOT here: this is a text-to-SQL
# repo, a .sql file is far more likely to be a fixture someone meant to
# commit, and silently ignoring that is its own defect.
*.dump
*.dump.*
*.backup
*.sql.gz
*.dump.gz
